## No AI Comments (All Languages)

Write no comment by default. People and agents read code well, so a comment earns its place only when it states a fact the code cannot carry: a hidden constraint, a subtle invariant, a workaround tied to a named bug, thread safety or lock ordering, or behavior a reader would otherwise misunderstand. This applies to every language you touch.

Never write:

- Narrative comments restating the code (`// Set the user`, `# increment counter`), including numbered steps that narrate the next block (`// 2. Run each member once`). If the steps matter, give them named functions.
- Section banners (`# ==== Helpers ====`) and labels that only name a field or block (`/// Rows per page.`, `# --- client`). Use names and file structure instead.
- Docstrings or doc-comments that paraphrase the signature of a function whose name already says what it does (`/** Get user by id */` above `getUserById`), one-line or multi-line. An `Args:`/`Returns:` section that repeats the parameter names is the same noise. When a lint or doc build requires one (`missing_docs`, a published API), state the contract the signature cannot: units, ranges, error modes, side effects.
- Context-of-origin comments (`# added for the payment flow`, `// handles issue #123`). That belongs in the commit message, not source.
- Change-history comments (`# was: old_value = 5`, `// renamed from oldFunc`). Use git.
- Multi-line rationale, design history or narration, even when it contains a reason. A comment states the one fact the code cannot; the story of how you got there goes in the PR description, or as one sentence in the commit body.

Heavily commented surrounding code is no reason to add more: match its naming and idiom, not its narration, and leave existing comments alone unless your edit changes what they describe.

If a pre-edit hook blocks a Write or Edit, delete the comment (most common), or rewrite it to state a reason the reader could not learn from the code; adding a word like "because" to narration does not make it a reason. The test: would a senior engineer reading cold learn something the code itself does not say?
